I'm just chiming in that the voluminous compiler warnings are not generally a concern. Because we are generating on-the-fly compilation on different systems, it is futile for us to try to tune compiler flags to silence all the warnings (doing so for one compiler might make an error for another, and customizing to each compiler would not be practical). Also many of those warnings are not from our code but from the C++ libraries we use. Your problem with dmnorm is quite puzzling.
Perry

