Thanks, Perry, for the reassurance concerning warnings :)

My 'showCompilerOutput=TRUE' run on the original minimal model can be seen above in this thread (for comparison), so I decided to simplify the model even further to provide another example. Here are two models, one with dnorm(), one with dmnorm():

mncode <- nimbleCode({
  y[1:n] ~ dmnorm(zeroes[1:n], cov = C[1:n, 1:n])
})

ncode <- nimbleCode({
  for(i in 1:n) {
    y[i] ~ dnorm(z, sd = S)
  }
})

n <- 5
mnmodel <- nimbleModel(mncode, constants = list(n = n, zeroes = rep(0, n), C = diag(n)), inits = list(y = rep(0, n)))
nmodel <- nimbleModel(ncode, constants = list(n = n, z = 0, S = 1), inits = list(y = rep(0, n)))

The (lengthy!) showCompilerOutput=TRUE outputs (with many warnings) for both models (first nmodel, then mnmodel) are shown at the end of this post.

Now something very mysterious happened. First time around, the 'nmodel' worked fine as expected (even though the showCompilerOutput=TRUE output for nmodel generates many warnings, see below). The showCompilerOutput=TRUE output for mnmodel also generated warnings (see below), but then R crashed when running
  mcmc <- nimbleMCMC(model = mnmodel,

  nchains = 1, niter = 1000, nburnin=10, thin=1,

  summary = TRUE, monitors = c('y'))
with the *uncompiled* mnmodel---again, this was expected. When I fired up R again, and tried using the *compiled* model instead,
(from cmnmodel<-compileNimble(mnmodel,showCompilerOutput = TRUE))
IT RAN OK!
Well, I thought I had pinpointed the problem, and just to make sure, I tried again with the *uncompiled' model that crashed before, and now it runs fine, too!
Boy, am I confused! Are my troubles over? I hope so, but am not overly confident, since I have no idea how the problem got "fixed". Anyway, now I'll go back to slightly more useful models, and report my success or failure.

Again, thanks for all your help and support!
Michael O'Dell

